A||N||3||fropuff||Blown indicator bulb??||||5||
Z||000000||fropuff||01-01-2001||01:35 PM||||When I was starting the Fiero yesterday, I noticed when I put on the left turn signal, the green indicator arrow didn't come on.  Did for the right side.  All the lights outside the car work; just not the arrow.  Is it a blown bulb?  How do I go replacing it?  Thanks.<P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who are worried about being caught.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Phaeton||01-01-2001||02:01 PM||||I've got my 88 instument cluster here on my lap.  All the bulbs are glued to 1/8 turn bases.  They twist right out and in with no slop.<BR>My cluster is already out of the Fiero, but I just don't see where there was room to get a hand back there to change the bulbs without pulling the cluster forward.<BR>I don't recall it being very difficult, four screws to loosen the panel and the wires had enough freeplay to pull the panel forward to access the rear cover screws.  Heck, maybe the back cover comes off without moving the cluster, didn't even try to do that.<P>Of course I work in a shop where a lot of underdash work is done and borrowed really fancy tools from the techs, specialty tools make any job seem easy. ||||reg||12
Z||000002||fropuff||01-01-2001||02:05 PM||||Okay, thanks.  So it is a bulb then?  Maybe I can fit my hand behind.  I'll try in the spring.  I won't start it much more anyway.  And it's too frickin' cold outside!  Thanks again.<P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who are worried about being caught.<p>[This message has been edited by fropuff (edited 01-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||01-01-2001||03:00 PM||||get to them from back, 3 screws on top and two 7 mm screws on bottom, just twist any of them a 1/4 turn||||reg||1
